{"timestamp": "2025-07-02T13:42:19.881800", "request_id": "08d424e4-f17b-4376-bae9-d805f2dcb88a", "nickname": "IamQueue", "model": "openai", "summary": "No, the method that best predicted synergy was not the method that best predicted antagonism. The best performers in predicting antagonism were almost random at predicting synergism, while those good at predicting synergism were almost random at predicting antagonism. This indicates that methods excelled in predicting one type of interaction (synergistic or antagonistic) over the other. The exception was the \"wisdom of the crowd\" solution, which was significant in predicting both synergistic and antagonistic interactions. This suggests trying to predict synergism and antagonism involves different mechanisms."}
{"timestamp": "2025-07-02T13:42:22.862336", "request_id": "08d424e4-f17b-4376-bae9-d805f2dcb88a", "nickname": "IamQueue", "model": "perplexity", "summary": "The method that best predicted synergy was **not** the same as the method that best predicted antagonism. The results showed that participants whose methods were statistically significant for predicting antagonism performed almost randomly when predicting synergy, and vice versa\u2014those strong at predicting synergy did poorly at predicting antagonism. The only exception was the \"wisdom of the crowd\" (an aggregate of all models), which achieved statistical significance in predicting both synergy and antagonism. This suggests that **methods effective for predicting synergy are generally not effective for predicting antagonism**, and each likely requires separate modeling approaches."}
