{"timestamp": "2025-07-02T19:01:59.494419", "request_id": "ed11524a-7438-482c-bc72-ef13a5c0febe", "nickname": "Bruno", "prompt": "Summarize the following talks key findings within 5 lines", "question_difficulty": "Easy", "file": "Talk1.pdf", "speaker": "Talk1", "text_preview": "p g\n\n\n## RESEARCH Open Access\n\n\n# Benchmarking foundation cell models for post\u2011perturbation RNA\u2011seq prediction\n\n#### Gerold Csendes[1], Gema Sanz[1], Krist\u00f3f Z. Szalay[1] and Bence Szalai[1*]\n\n\n**Abst..."}
{"timestamp": "2025-07-02T19:02:02.481562", "request_id": "ed11524a-7438-482c-bc72-ef13a5c0febe", "nickname": "Bruno", "model": "openai", "summary": "The study benchmarks two advanced machine learning models, scGPT and scFoundation, for predicting RNA-seq post-perturbation profiles but finds that simpler baseline models perform better. Simple models, like one using the mean of training examples, surprisingly outperformed both scGPT and scFoundation. The Random Forest Regressor, using Gene Ontology features, showed superior performance compared to foundational models. It was identified that current datasets used for benchmarking have limited variability, which diminishes their effectiveness in evaluating model performance. Thus, the research suggests that datasets with more diverse biological variability are required to better assess predictive models in this domain."}
{"timestamp": "2025-07-02T19:02:17.418590", "request_id": "ed11524a-7438-482c-bc72-ef13a5c0febe", "nickname": "Bruno", "model": "perplexity", "summary": "1. The study benchmarked two foundation models, scGPT and scFoundation, for post-perturbation RNA-seq prediction and found that even the simplest baseline, averaging training samples (Train Mean), outperformed them in most cases.\n2. Machine learning models that incorporated biologically meaningful features, especially a Random Forest Regressor using Gene Ontology (GO) terms, performed substantially better than the tested foundation models.\n3. The analysis showed that commonly used Perturb-Seq benchmark datasets display low perturbation-specific variance, which limits their ability to differentiate between models\u2019 performances.\n4. The limited biological variance in these datasets allows trivial models to achieve high scores and complicates meaningful evaluation of new, more complex models.\n5. The findings suggest that more diverse and representative benchmarking datasets are essential for accurately assessing and improving machine learning models in post-perturbation gene expression prediction."}
