{"timestamp": "2025-07-02T19:04:48.250072", "request_id": "30e67194-c4b1-4af4-a07e-ebd2bf894e02", "nickname": "Bruno", "model": "openai", "summary": "Julio Saez-Rodriguez discussed the role of computational models in personalized medicine, emphasizing the utilization of large-scale omics data to understand disease mechanisms and predict therapy responses. His group integrates biological knowledge with machine learning to enhance the interpretability and accuracy of these models. Saez-Rodriguez stresses combining omics data with dynamic models to simulate cellular responses to perturbations over time. This approach can better predict drug responses and enables targeted therapeutics by constructing organ and disease-specific models. He also introduced tools and methods like Omnipath and Cosmos for integrating and analyzing biological data within this framework."}
{"timestamp": "2025-07-02T19:04:48.320899", "request_id": "30e67194-c4b1-4af4-a07e-ebd2bf894e02", "nickname": "Bruno", "model": "perplexity", "summary": "Julio Saez-Rodriguez\u2019s talk highlighted that integrating **biological knowledge with machine learning models** enhances the interpretation and predictive performance for personalized medicine. His group developed tools, like Omnipath and Cosmos, to extract mechanistic, interpretable features from large-scale multi-omics data. These methods identify pathway and transcription factor activities, map causal networks, and generate hypotheses about disease and therapy response. While machine learning alone offers limited predictive performance, leveraging curated biological information and building dynamic models of cellular response to perturbations significantly improves predictions and mechanistic insight. However, predicting drug responses remains challenging due to data and model limitations, and experimental validation is essential for clinical translation[3]."}
