{"timestamp": "2025-07-02T19:18:39.061627", "request_id": "514b89f3-15bf-4f83-9d18-25be94e21056", "nickname": "Arivederci", "model": "perplexity", "summary": "There are **10 highly homologous APOBEC enzymes** in humans, as described in the provided transcript[1]. These enzymes are part of a family that evolved through duplication of an ancestral gene on chromosome 22, and they all share a highly homologous cytidine deaminase domain[1]. This family includes several members such as APOBEC3A, APOBEC3B, and others, with their main biological roles in mutating RNA or DNA, particularly as part of the innate immune response[1]. While some sources discuss the broader AID/APOBEC family as having 11 members, the transcript specifically mentions a family of 10 highly similar enzymes in humans[1]."}
